  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1251 AM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nA warm front is lifting north of the FA this morning as a broad \nupper level ridge begins to build more east with drier conditions. \nPatchy fog remains possible with mostly clear skies; otherwise, \nsoutherly return flow will increase the heat and moisture each day \nas a warm sector regime dominates through the first half of the \nweek. As a result, a Heat Advisory is now in effect this afternoon \nthrough Wednesday evening for heat index values of 100 to 108 \ndegrees. While most of the period through Tuesday will be dry, \nthe one caveat for tonight into Monday morning is the ARW which \nshows the potential for a complex of storms to move downstream \nfrom central Illinois. At the moment this is an outlier with \nmost model guidance including the NBM keeping any rain chances \nnorth of the FA. \n\nAs the ridge of high pressure begins to breakdown in the latter half \nof the week, daily shower and thunderstorm chances around 40-60% \nmake a return on Wednesday with a series of shortwaves moving \nacross the Ohio Valley. Relief from the heat eventually arrives,\nas a cold front sags southeast and becomes stationary over the \nFA late Thursday into Thursday night. In the wake of the front, \nsome of the heat headlines may eventually need to be extended \ninto Thursday for portions of the FA contingent on timing. With \nan increase in wind shear and sufficient instability Wednesday \nand especially Thursday, a few stronger to potentially severe \nstorms will be possible. Heavy rainfall will also be another \nhazard of concern with PWATs exceeding 2 inches.",
